title A record read back out of the box, and a ledger that ended every count at the first if block
prompt The baton, unattended lap 4091 on grain-grass. Rota row 1 by count, which my own last lap read, so I advanced by hand to row 2, which is Fire.
rota lap N 4091, N mod 5 = 1 repeats my last lap, advanced by hand to row 2, Fire -- Mars, cut and stop; threshold fire-the-row-that-sees, then standfast-the-stopped-line, reds-first-and-the-allocation, the-three-depths-of-removal
think Fire's instruction is to read the ledger and watch what the witnesses refuse before reading anything new. The round open said it plainly on its first line: one session log stands in the dead-letter box and nowhere else. That is a red in my own tree, so it took the allocation ahead of the work I had prepared.
think The stash held a whole lap of my own seat -- 8 tracked files, 3 untracked, 420 insertions, a 155-line control and a finished session log. Its code had never landed either: the elder shell glob still stood in the roster, the guard was still absent from construction/standing-equipment.kyri, and no control existed. So it was unlanded rather than duplicated, and it was mine to read back out.
think I took the content rather than the diff. The parked lap wrote its ITINERARY, REDS and roster edits against a HEAD two commits back, and both pins have moved since; applying that diff would have meant resolving conflicts in exactly the three living pins where a wrong resolution is least visible. Checking the guard files out of the stash and rewriting the three pin edits by hand on today's bytes is the same landing with none of the conflict.
think The number had to move. The parked lap booked %506 from a local read and %506 stands published upstream for a different defect, so under the derived spine an unshared row renumbers to the fetched head: 508. Its own session log cited the old number in two places and is testimony that never shipped, so it was corrected before it landed rather than after. *(Corrected again `20260906.194500`: this lap died before its own commit too, %508 and %509 went to peers in the gap, and lap 4095 read both records back out and landed them at %511 and %512.)*
think My own prepared find is the second half. The card named it as an unclaimed sibling: tame_style_long_fn_one.sh resets on any indented fn. Measuring rather than agreeing found a second and larger hole beside it, and the two together are why TAME's own 70-line ratchet has read a clean tree.
obs A closing-brace pattern cannot end a function, because the brace that closes a function and the brace that closes its first block are the same character at the same column. The elder awk ended a function at the first line matching ^}$ or ^    }$; the second alternative fits a struct method, whose body closes at four spaces, and a free function's first if block closes at four spaces too. Over the roster's 611 files it reported 15 functions past 70 lines where 321 stand.
obs The scale surprised me and I checked it by hand rather than trusting my own counter. caravan/farewell.rye:check_suffice_runs opens at line 10768 and its matching brace is at 11576, which is 809 lines; the elder's longest reading anywhere was 180. Ten functions past 600 lines were invisible. pond/apps/scooter_paint.rye:run_selftest opens at 283 and closes at 606, which is 324 where the elder read 127.
obs Every function the elder found is still found, ten of the fifteen at exactly the same count. That is the reading that made me trust the replacement: a counter that disagreed everywhere would more likely be wrong than the one it replaced.
obs Counting braces introduces a hazard the elder never had, since the elder counted no braces at all. Five roster files carry a brace inside a char literal and twelve carry 119 multiline-string lines holding one, and a format string with {d} is on nearly every page. So the strip function scans each line character by character rather than sweeping it with a regex, and four control legs exist only to hold that new mechanism rather than to bite the elder.
obs I gated the instrument and not the count. 321 standing functions belong to six lanes at once and TAME's own tidy rules seat this family as a ratchet that prints rather than fails, so a ceiling here would refuse other ships' ordinary work for a defect this lap did not create. What is gated is the control, the absence of both elder patterns from the code, and a floor of one on the tree's own reading -- because a ledger reporting zero across 611 files has gone blind, and blindness prints what health prints.
obs The witness would have red on its own explanation. Its two text readings grep for the elder patterns, and my new header quotes both of them on purpose, so the first draft found them in the comment and refused. The readings strip comment lines now; a guard that reds on its own documentation teaches the next hand to delete the documentation.
obs My control caught three of my own arithmetic errors on its first run, and one of them was a leg that could not fail: grep -q on empty input returns 1, so a check for "no output" written as a grep for the empty string reported FAIL on a clean read rather than passing. Silence is a reading and it wants a string test.
obs The ledger runner printed ten rows and no total, which is %505's lesson in a second room: an unprinted reach is a reach claimed by implication. It prints functions_over_70 and longest now, and the witness's blindness floor reads that line.
obs Rishi refused my first ledger runner with StringTooLong, and the bound was right. The elder orchestration joined every per-file chunk into one string and interpolated it into a shell command to rank it; at 15 rows that fits and at 321 it does not. So the walk moved into tame_style_long_fn_scan.sh, which walks once, prints a summary line ahead of the top ten, and hands rishi a reading small enough to hold -- the scan-plus-witness shape every other guard family here already uses, and 611 fewer subprocess spawns.
obs The doorway ratchet read 46 against the parked lap's ceiling of 45, because one external-research page landed between that measurement and this send. A ceiling only falls from what it PUBLISHES, and this row publishes for the first time, so it ships at the reading taken at the send rather than at a number measured on a tree that has since moved -- %341's borrowed-number class, one room over.
obs Two of the 46 read **Status:** Proposed -- external research, and the matcher is right to refuse them: TWO_ROOMS.md's gloss table names checkable, vision, mixed, and research for understanding, and proposed is among none of them. Its own prose says what is proven as proven and what is proposed as proposed. That is the canon disagreeing with its own table, which is Keaton's to settle rather than a guard's, so the pages are counted honestly and the question goes on the card.
obs My own two shelves landed in a room already past its bound: construction/archive stands at 309 flat files against 256, undated and advisory, so the scan's verdict stays ok and nothing refuses. It was over before this lap and is two files further over now. Naming it rather than folding it mid-round -- an archive fold is a lap with its own reference sweep, and archive is a closed stack.
obs I reached for pkill -f "standing_equipment_run.sh --hot" to stop a pass I had voided by editing a file under it, and that pattern is machine-wide: eight ships share this pier, so it would have killed a peer's hot pass had one been running. None was, by the process listing before and after, and the honest reading is that I was lucky rather than careful. A pass is stopped by the PID the launch returned, never by a pattern that matches every tree.
obs And the reason I had to stop it at all is worth keeping: the runner digests the tree at open and close and refuses tree_moved when they differ, so a single log edit after git add voids twenty minutes. The rule is not merely do not edit the runner -- it is that a hot pass is the LAST thing a round does, after every byte is written.
loom scan=tame_style_long_fn roster_files=611 elder_reported=15 depth_reported=321 files_with_a_long_fn=173 longest_elder=180 longest_depth=809 over_600=10 exact_agreements=10 walk_s=19
loom control=tame_style_long_fn legs=20 green_repaired=20 green_under_elder=17 bitten=3
loom scan=stash_record stashes=2 records=2 unlanded_before=1 unlanded_after=0
loom cold_pass=scoped guards_reported=122 reds=1 red_name=stash_record skipped_scope=30 skipped_capability=2
